WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
Microsoft Entra ID is the current name for Azure AD.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:14.360
It provides identity and access management services for users, groups, applications, service principals, workloads, and tenants.

3
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:23.420
For .NET developers, it is a central part of authentication and access architecture, but it is not the entire security design by itself.

4
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:29.620
A tenant is an identity boundary for an organization or customer environment.

5
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:34.120
Users and groups represent human identities and collections of those identities.

6
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:38.780
App registrations describe how software integrates with the Microsoft identity platform.

7
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:45.200
Enterprise applications and service principals represent application instances inside tenants.

8
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.500
Resource APIs expose protected capabilities.

9
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.840
Client applications request tokens.

10
00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:56.460
Workload identities represent non-human software that needs to authenticate to other services.

11
00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:05.060
Entra ID can authenticate a user, application, or workload and issue tokens that express facts about that authentication event.

12
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.880
The .NET application still has responsibilities.

13
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:21.340
It must validate tokens using maintained components, enforce authorization for application actions, protect credentials and token caches, interpret claims carefully, and log useful events without recording sensitive values.

14
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.500
The trust boundary depends on the application type.

15
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:35.960
Internal web apps, public customer-facing apps, partner portals, APIs, daemon services, background jobs, and administrative tools all have different assumptions.

16
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:42.140
Internal network location or a familiar tenant name should not be treated as proof that every request is trusted.

17
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.560
The application still needs server-side policy and resource checks.

18
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:54.200
A secure Entra ID design separates identity provider responsibility from application responsibility.

19
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.180
The identity platform authenticates and issues tokens according to tenant policy.

20
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:09.680
The application validates those tokens, decides what the caller may do, protects its own configuration and secrets, and produces operational evidence for review.
